    "q-132421": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132421",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy.$",
      "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[x]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy & =[-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }-(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
