    "q-132562": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132562",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14220353/assets/d5f54020da277980a58151bf2a85911365e67ecae6832881e03ac8b9b6431e6a.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}4x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $16{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ above the $x$-axis, as shown in the picture.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}} & =\\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}} \\\\ 1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our region lies above the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14220353/assets/d8b8b05e9004f6b25f635e9c39421a8e8e27831a26a22273cf88572df89ffc45.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}4x\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,4x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,4x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,2x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,2{[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]}_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,2[4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})]dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,[3-\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,(1-\\frac{1}{16}{y}^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})dy & =6{[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{48}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =6(4-\\frac{64}{48}) \\\\  & =6(4-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
